-- title:
--   The cone gap vanishes at the apex
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,0) = 0,$$
--
--   where $\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) = \langle v,p\rangle\sin\vartheta - \lVert p - \langle v,p\rangle v\rVert\cos\vartheta$ is the signed distance from $p$ to the boundary of the half-cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$.
--
--   The apex of the cone has no clearance at all, as it lies on the boundary. Trivial as it is, this normalization is what pins down the sign convention (the gap is strictly positive exactly on the open cone, and the apex is excluded) and it is the base case of the homogeneity and translation identities.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypotheses at all are imposed: neither $\lVert v\rVert = 1$ nor any constraint on $\vartheta$. The lemma is tagged `@[simp]`, so it is applied automatically during simplification.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L126-L127

